[# Section 0 — Quickstart. Front-matter; appears before the §1 intro.
   Authored 2026-05-22.
   Goal: a developer who has never seen CX is running CX in under five
   minutes. Cross-links use [[anchor]] form; spec references use
   ordinary markdown links. File extension .cxd — data-only source. #]

[section id=quickstart group='Start here' n=0 title='Quickstart — first 5 minutes with CX'

  [intro
    """This is the shortest path from "I have never seen CX" to "I have
       parsed, hashed, queried, and validated my first document, and
       called CX from my host language." Eight steps, five minutes if
       your toolchain is warm. If you only have time for one section,
       read this one — then jump to [[next-steps]]."""]

  [child id=install n=0.1 title='Install'
    [intro
      """CX ships as a single self-contained binary `cx` plus a
         shared library `libcx` for host-language bindings. On macOS
         (Apple Silicon) the primary install path is the release
         installer:"""]

    [example lang=shell
      """curl -sSL https://cxhome.org/install | sh
         cx --version"""]

    [body
      """The installer resolves the latest release and installs the
         `platform` profile by default; leaner compositions install
         with `CX_PROFILE=data`, `CX_PROFILE=embed`, or
         `CX_PROFILE=cli`. Every profile tarball is linked from the
         guide's downloads page. Prebuilt tarballs currently cover
         macOS (Apple Silicon) only; a Homebrew tap and a Docker
         image are not published."""]

    [body
      """**Build from source** — the alternative everywhere, and the
         only path on Linux and Windows. devbox pins the host
         toolchains (Python, Rust); the V toolchain comes from the
         vendored fork at `third_party/v`, a git submodule, so clone
         with `--recursive`:"""]

    [example lang=shell
      """git clone --recursive https://github.com/cx-home/cx
         cd cx
         devbox run -- make build-vcx
         devbox run -- make install-cli   [# → /usr/local/bin/cx; PREFIX= overrides #]
         cx --version"""]

    [body
      """Per-platform notes — loader paths, editor wiring, the
         WebAssembly bundle — are in §0.10."""]

    [note
      """If `cx --version` prints a version line, you are ready.
         If you see `command not found`, your `PATH` does not include
         the install location — see [[faq]] for recovery steps."""]]

  [child id=hello-cx n=0.2 title='Hello, CX'
    [intro
      """Every interactive CX session starts the same way: pipe a CX
         literal into the `cx` binary and see what comes out."""]

    [example lang=shell
      """echo '[hello greeting="world"]' | cx"""]

    [body
      """The output is the same shape you typed:"""]

    [example lang=cx
      """[hello greeting=world]"""]

    [body
      """Three things to notice. **First**, the value evaluated to
         itself — data and code share one syntax, and a bare data
         element is its own value (see [[homoiconicity]]). **Second**,
         the double quotes are gone — canonical output drops quotes
         entirely when a token is bare-safe, and attribute order is
         preserved as written; attributes are never re-sorted, not
         even for hashing (see [[canonical]]).
         **Third**, no closing tag duplication — every CX element is a
         single bracketed form. That is the whole syntax."""]

    [body
      """Try a tiny program too. The `[?for]` directive iterates and
         yields, one line per yielded item:"""]

    [example lang=shell
      """echo '[?for [in $x (1, 2, 3)] [yield [n $x]]]' | cx"""]

    [example lang=cx
      """[n 1]
         [n 2]
         [n 3]"""]]

  [child id=first-file n=0.3 title='Your first file'
    [intro
      """Real CX work lives in files. Create `users.cx` with three
         users:"""]

    [example lang=cx
      """[users
           [user id=1 email='ada@example.com'    active=true]
           [user id=2 email='grace@example.com'  active=false]
           [user id=3 email='linus@example.com'  active=true]]"""]

    [body
      """**Parse it** (round-trips through the canonicalizer; useful
         as a syntax check):"""]

    [example lang=shell
      """cx fmt users.cx              [# lossless canonical — one line, double quotes, comments kept #]
         cx canonical users.cx        [# strict canonical bytes — indented, quotes dropped where bare-safe #]"""]

    [body
      """**Hash it** — the SHA-256 of the strict canonical bytes is
         the document's identity (see [[hash]]):"""]

    [example lang=shell
      """cx hash users.cx
         [# → sha2-256:3ace03ec...7a13c9e6  (tagged; 64 hex chars)     #]"""]

    [body
      """The hash is stable across machines, OS versions, and
         bindings. Two documents with the same hash are
         byte-identical in canonical form."""]]

  [child id=first-conversion n=0.4 title='Convert JSON ↔ CX'
    [intro
      """CX speaks JSON (and YAML, TOML, XML, Markdown) as
         projections. JSON, YAML, TOML, and XML round-trip losslessly
         under the `--lossless` flag; the default JSON projection is
         idiomatic and can drop CX-only detail such as mixed content;
         Markdown and CSV are lossy by design. Start from JSON:"""]

    [example lang=shell
      """cat > users.json <<'EOF'
         {"users":[
           {"id":1,"email":"ada@example.com","active":true},
           {"id":2,"email":"grace@example.com","active":false}
         ]}
         EOF
         cx --from=json --to=cx users.json > users.cx"""]

    [body
      """`users.cx` now contains the CX projection of that JSON.
         Convert back and verify the round-trip:"""]

    [example lang=shell
      """cx --from=cx --to=json users.cx > users.roundtrip.json
         diff <(jq -S . users.json) <(jq -S . users.roundtrip.json)
         [# → no output: byte-identical after canonicalization        #]"""]

    [body
      """The document's identity is also stable through a lossless
         projection round-trip — `cx hash users.cx` equals
         `cx --from=json --to=cx users.json | cx hash` — because
         hashing is computed on canonical bytes (see
         [[canon-cross-format]]). The lossy lanes (default JSON for
         mixed content, Markdown, CSV) are the named exceptions."""]]

  [child id=first-selection n=0.5 title='Select with CXPath'
    [intro
      """CXPath is the universal selector — for reading, for
         iteration, for update focus. Same vocabulary everywhere. The
         simplest query asks: "which users are active?""""]

    [body
      """A CX program is just another document, so the quickest CLI
         query appends one to your data and runs the result — the
         program's `//` paths resolve against the document that
         precedes it:"""]

    [example lang=shell
      """echo '[?for [in $u //user[= $_@active true]] [yield $u@email]]' \
           | cat users.cx - | cx"""]

    [body
      """Read the predicate as: "from anywhere in the document (`//`),
         find a `user` element whose `@active` attribute equals `true`,
         then take its `@email` attribute." Predicates are ordinary
         prefix CX code over the context node `$_` — `[= $_@active
         true]`. If you have written XPath, the alignment is
         deliberate — see the CXPath sections of
         [`spec/03-approved/core/code.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/code.md)
         and the conformance corpus `conformance/xpath_31_parity.cxd`
         for what carries over and what does not."""]

    [body
      """The same path drives iteration in a program:"""]

    [example lang=cx
      """[users
           [user email='ada@example.com' active=true]
           [user email='bob@example.com' active=false]]
         [?for [in $u //user[= $_@active true]] [yield
           [active-user email=$u@email]]]"""]]

  [child id=first-validation n=0.6 title='Validate with a schema'
    [intro
      """A CX schema (`.cxs`) is a CX document that describes the
         allowed shape of another CX document. Write
         `users.cxs`:"""]

    [example lang=cx
      """[schema of=users mode=strict]
         [users [elem user [card "1..*"]]]
         [user
           [attr id::int [req]]
           [attr email::string [req]]
           [attr active::bool [req]]]"""]

    [body
      """Run the validator:"""]

    [example lang=shell
      """cx validate users.cx --schema=users.cxs
         [# → prints nothing; exit code 0                              #]"""]

    [body
      """Break it on purpose to see a diagnostic. Remove `active`
         from one user and re-run:"""]

    [example lang=shell
      """cx validate users.cx --schema=users.cxs
         [# → users.cx:2:4: error: S002: missing required attribute 'active' on <user>  #]"""]

    [body
      """Schema modes are `open` / `strict` / `closed` — strict
         rejects unknown attributes, closed also rejects unknown
         child elements. See [[schema]] for the full constraint
         vocabulary."""]]

  [child id=first-binding n=0.7 title='Hello from your host language'
    [intro
      """CX ships four Tier-1 host-language bindings — V (the native
         reference), Python, Go, and Rust. The Layer-1 method set is
         identical across all four (see [[layer-1]]). One install,
         one shape:"""]

    [body
      """**Python:**"""]

    [example lang=shell
      """pip install cxlib   [# when published — pre-publish: pip install -e lang/python/ #]"""]

    [example lang=python
      """import cxlib

         with open('users.cx', 'rb') as f:
             doc = cxlib.Doc.parse(f.read())
         print(doc.hash())                       [# tagged sha2-256:… #]
         for user in doc.select_all('//user[= $_@active true]'):
             print(user.attr('email'))"""]

    [body
      """**V** (native — imports the V core directly):"""]

    [example lang=v
      """import os
         import native as cx

         doc := cx.parse_doc(os.read_file('users.cx')!)!
         println(doc.hash()!)
         for user in doc.select_all('//user[= $_@active true]')! {
             println(user.attr('email') or { continue })
         }"""]

    [body
      """**Go:**"""]

    [example lang=go
      """import "github.com/cx-home/cx/lang/go/cxlib"

         src, _ := os.ReadFile("users.cx")
         doc, _ := cxlib.ParseDoc(src)
         h, _ := doc.Hash()
         fmt.Println(h)
         users, _ := doc.SelectAll("//user[= $_@active true]")
         for _, u := range users {
             fmt.Println(u.Attr("email"))
         }"""]

    [body
      """**Rust:**"""]

    [example lang=rust
      """use cxlib::code::Doc;

         let doc = Doc::parse(&std::fs::read("users.cx")?)?;
         println!("{}", doc.hash()?);
         for user in doc.select_all("//user[= $_@active true]")? {
             println!("{}", user.attr("email").unwrap());
         }"""]

    [note
      """The identity-critical operations — hashing,
         canonicalization, program eval — are one implementation (the
         V core that `libcx` is compiled from), so their results are
         bit-identical across bindings. Parsing and several emitters
         are implemented natively per binding and held to parity by
         the conformance corpus: the Layer-1 parity gate in
         [`spec/03-approved/misc/bindings.md`](../../spec/03-approved/misc/bindings.md),
         tracked per binding in
         [`spec/03-approved/misc/parity-matrix.md`](../../spec/03-approved/misc/parity-matrix.md)."""]]

  [child id=next-steps n=0.8 title='Where to go next'
    [intro
      """You now have CX installed, you can write data, hash it,
         convert it, query it, validate it, and call it from your
         host language. From here, follow your interest:"""]

    [list
      [item """**Learn the data language end-to-end.** [[intro]]
              walks the design philosophy; [[data]] is the
              full reference for scalars, collections, sigils, and
              mixed content."""]
      [item """**Learn the code language.** [[code]] covers the
              directive registry, CXPath in depth, patterns, builtins,
              services / workers / async, and resilience."""]
      [item """**Wire a binding into a real project.** [[bindings]]
              has per-language quickstarts and the Layer-1 / Layer-2
              method set."""]
      [item """**Pick a tooling integration.** [[tooling]] covers
              the LSP, tree-sitter grammar, VS Code, Neovim, Helix,
              `cx lint`, `cx diff`, and the playground."""]
      [item """**Migrate an existing codebase.** [[migration]]
              covers prior-version migration and intake from
              JSON / YAML / TOML / XML."""]
      [item """**Compare against alternatives.** [[comparison]]
              is the side-by-side against JSON, YAML, TOML, XML,
              Protobuf, MessagePack, Pkl, Dhall, Parquet, and
              Arrow — including where CX is the wrong choice."""]
      [item """**Look up a term.** [[glossary]] defines every
              CX-specific term used in this guide; [[faq]] covers
              the first-week-of-CX questions."""]]

    [note
      """The community lives at https://github.com/cx-home/cx — file
         bugs and read the spec: approved documents live under
         `spec/03-approved/`, in-flight work under `spec/02-working/`.
         The guide ships an in-browser playground (`make guide` renders
         it to `docs/guide/playground.html`) that runs CX with no
         install."""]]

  [# Migrated from docs-src/content/{quickstart,install,tutorial}.cx
     during Phase B docs → guide consolidation (2026-05-24).
     Designer pre-approved §0.9 "Twenty-chapter walkthrough" as a
     new front-matter sub-section. Install detail is folded as
     §0.10. Quickstart angles fold into the existing §0.1-§0.8
     subsections; this batch only adds the unique tutorial track
     and the per-platform install detail. #]

  [child id=install-detail n=0.10 title='Install — per-platform detail'
    [intro
      """Per-platform install instructions. Section §0.1 covers the
         installer and source paths at a glance; this section
         carries the full per-platform breakdown including loader
         path and editor wiring."""]

    [child id=install-status n=0.10.1 title='Release status'
      [body
        """Every release publishes prebuilt macOS (Apple Silicon)
           tarballs — one per profile (`platform`, `cli`, `embed`,
           `data`) — plus the installer one-liner
           `curl -sSL https://cxhome.org/install | sh`. Linux and
           Windows have no prebuilts and build from source; a
           Homebrew tap and a Docker image are not published.
           Production users should pin to a release tag."""]]

    [child id=install-macos n=0.10.2 title='macOS'
      [example lang=shell
        """curl -sSL https://cxhome.org/install | sh"""]
      [body
        """Installs the prebuilt Apple Silicon `platform` profile;
           `CX_PROFILE=data|embed|cli` selects a leaner one. Source
           builds work on Apple Silicon and Intel
           (`devbox run -- make build-vcx && devbox run -- make install-cli`).
           A Homebrew tap is not published."""]]

    [child id=install-linux n=0.10.3 title='Linux'
      [body
        """Build from source; the result is a static binary that
           runs on any glibc-based distro. No prebuilt Linux tarball
           is published."""]
      [example lang=shell
        """git clone --recursive https://github.com/cx-home/cx && cd cx
           devbox run -- make build-vcx && devbox run -- make install-cli"""]]

    [child id=install-windows n=0.10.4 title='Windows'
      [body
        """A static `cx.exe` is planned per release on
           github.com/cx-home/cx/releases, but is not published yet
           (the V toolchain's Windows support is still on the
           roadmap — see [[faq]]); build from source in the
           meantime. Drop the binary on PATH; no installer, no
           dependencies."""]]

    [child id=install-wasm n=0.10.5 title='WebAssembly bundle'
      [body
        """The `libcx-wasm` bundle powers the live evaluator in
           the playground; it exports the
           `_cx_code_eval` family plus `_cx_code_diagram` and
           `_cx_code_tree`. The bundle ships
           alongside the `cx` binary on release tags."""]]

    [child id=install-source n=0.10.6 title='Build from source'
      [example lang=shell
        """git clone https://github.com/cx-home/cx
           cd cx
           devbox run -- make build-vcx
           devbox run -- make test"""]
      [body
        """The build chain expects V on PATH or the `cx-home` V
           fork at `third_party/v`; the Makefile auto-detects
           either. Check out the latest release tag to build
           a specific release."""]]

    [child id=install-verify n=0.10.7 title='Verify the install'
      [example lang=shell
        """cx --version
           cx --json - <<< "[hello world]" """]
      [note
        """A successful run prints the version and a JSON document
           on stdout. The `cx` binary is self-contained — it does not
           link `libcx`. If a host-language *binding* cannot find
           `libcx`, point it at the library with `LIBCX_PATH` (full
           path to the library file) or `LIBCX_LIB_DIR` (its
           directory)."""]]]

  [child id=tutorial-walkthrough n=0.9 title='Twenty-chapter walkthrough'
    [intro
      """This walkthrough walks CX from the smallest piece to the
         fullest expression in twenty linked chapters. Each
         chapter introduces one shape, shows it in context, and
         ends with two or three exercises you can run with `cx
         --ast` or `cx --json`. Pace yourself. Assumed background:
         familiarity with at least one of JSON, YAML, TOML, or XML
         — CX assumes you know what an object, array, scalar, and
         attribute are."""]

    [child id=tut-1 n=0.9.1 title='Why CX looks the way it does'
      [body
        """Most projects need two kinds of file: typed
           configuration in nested structures, and documents with
           prose plus structure. JSON, YAML, and TOML are
           optimized for the first; Markdown, HTML, and XML for
           the second. Projects end up with multiple file formats
           and multiple parsers. CX is one syntax for both. Five
           design constraints picked the shape: one construct, no
           indentation semantics, optional quoting, types
           first-class, lossless conversion to existing
           formats."""]]

    [child id=tut-2 n=0.9.2 title='Elements'
      [body
        """Every CX node is a bracket pair with a name and an
           optional body. Editors balance brackets natively; code
           folding works without parser hooks. The opening name
           appears once; nothing repeats at close."""]
      [example lang=cx """[p Hello, World]"""]
      [note
        """**Exercises.** Save as `hello.cx` and run `cx --ast
           hello.cx`. Add a child element and re-run. Then run `cx
           --json` on the same file."""]]

    [child id=tut-3 n=0.9.3 title='Attributes'
      [body
        """Attributes are name=value pairs after the element name.
           Bare values auto-type: `8080` becomes int, `3.14`
           becomes float, `true` becomes bool, `2026-01-01`
           becomes date. Leading-zero tokens stay strings —
           `02134` stays the string `02134` — to avoid the YAML
           Norway problem."""]
      [example lang=cx """[server host=api.example.com port=8080 ssl=true]"""]
      [note """**Exercises.** Add three more attributes and check what type cx assigns each. Quote a value containing a space and inspect the AST."""]]

    [child id=tut-4 n=0.9.4 title='Types'
      [body
        """CX auto-types most values; for the rest, a glued `::T`
           ascription pins the value or body type. Sized variants
           carry range constraints that validate at parse time."""]
      [example lang=cx """[port::u16 8080]"""]
      [note """**Exercises.** Try setting the `::u16` value to `70000` and observe the error. Compare `[n 12]` vs `[n::decimal 12]` in `cx --ast`."""]]

    [child id=tut-5 n=0.9.5 title='Element bodies'
      [body
        """A body holds Text, Scalar, child Element, or collection
           literal in source order. Mixed content is first-class
           — text and structure interleave the way they do in
           XML."""]
      [example lang=cx """[para Try the [b Margherita] — our [em best seller], hand-tossed daily.]"""]
      [note """Triple-quoted bodies preserve multi-line content verbatim. **Exercises.** Write an article with `h1`, `p`, `em`, `a` children; convert with `cx --md`."""]]

    [child id=tut-6 n=0.9.6 title='Comments'
      [body
        """Two comment forms, both valid at document level and inside
           element bodies. `#` starts a line comment; `[; … ]` is a
           block comment that can span lines."""]
      [example lang=cx """# top pick this week
         [shop port=8080
           [; the menu is trimmed for the tutorial ]
           [menu [pizza name=Margherita]]]"""]
      [note """**Exercises.** Check what `cx fmt` preserves and what `cx canonical` drops."""]]

    [child id=tut-7 n=0.9.7 title='Sigils'
      [body
        """Five sigils give CX structural reach beyond name and
           attribute. `@` is an attribute prefix in path
           expressions; in body position it appears only inside the
           `[ref @id]` reference form. `&`
           declares an anchor; `*` merges from an anchor; `#`
           declares an id; `:` pins a data type."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[defaults &shared timeout=30 retries=3]
           [server *shared name=api]"""]]

    [child id=tut-8 n=0.9.8 title='Collection literals'
      [body
        """Three first-class collection shapes. Sequence with
           parens flattens; array with square brackets preserves
           nesting; map with braces gives named key-to-value
           entries. Collections live in an element body —
           attributes hold a single scalar, never a collection."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[pizza [toppings (cheese, basil, oil)]]
           [pizza [prices {small: 9, medium: 12, large: 15}]]"""]]

    [child id=tut-9 n=0.9.9 title='Typed tables'
      [body
        """A `[table[…]]` block declares column names and types.
           The cells that follow partition into rows by the declared
           column count — newlines are ordinary whitespace, so one
           row per line is a readability convention, not a rule.
           Cells parse against the declared column types, and the
           table round-trips to CSV with types preserved via the
           header row. In the binary ast-bin form, the table record
           stores rows row-major; the column-major layout is the
           separate CXCol format (see [[analytics]])."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[orders [table[item::string qty::u32 paid::bool when::date]]
             Margherita 2 true  2026-05-09
             Hawaiian   1 false 2026-05-09]"""]]

    [child id=tut-10 n=0.9.10 title='Multi-document files'
      [body
        """A single CX file can hold multiple top-level documents
           separated by three dashes on a line by themselves.
           Same separator as YAML; each side parses as an
           independent document, and `cx --json` emits the file as
           an array of documents."""]]

    [child id=tut-11 n=0.9.11 title='Six surface dialects'
      [body
        """The same tree parses and emits as any of six document
           dialects — CX, XML, JSON, YAML, TOML, or Markdown. Each
           dialect captures the subset of the data model it can
           represent. Beyond the six, the delimited surfaces
           (CSV / TSV / PSV) and the columnar binary surface (CXCol)
           convert through the same codec layer — see
           [[analytics]]."""]
      [example lang=shell """cx --yaml pizza.cx"""]]

    [child id=tut-12 n=0.9.12 title='CX code — directives inside data'
      [body
        """CX code is CX that does things. A directive is an
           element whose name starts with `?`. Directives parse
           as regular CX and evaluate against the surrounding
           tree when `cx` runs the document."""]
      [example lang=cx """[page title='Menu — New Haven']
         [?let [= $t //page/@title] [h1 [$string $t]]]"""]]

    [child id=tut-13 n=0.9.13 title='Path expressions (CXPath)'
      [body
        """CXPath is the query language inside CX code. Paths
           separated by `/`; predicates in `[…]` are prefix CX code
           over the context node `$_`; axes navigate by
           relationship."""]
      [example lang=cx """[menu [pizza name=Margherita price=12]
               [pizza name=Marinara price=9]]
         //pizza[> $_@price 10]"""]]

    [child id=tut-14 n=0.9.14 title='Let and for'
      [body
        """`[?let]` binds a name once and reuses it. `[?for]`
           iterates a path expression result."""]
      [example lang=cx """[?let [= $tax 0.22] [= $price 12] [* $price [+ 1 $tax]]]"""]]

    [child id=tut-15 n=0.9.15 title='Filters'
      [body
        """Filter functions transform values. Compose them with
           `[?pipe]` — bare stages, threaded left to right — or nest
           them."""]
      [example lang=cx """[?lib 'cx-stdlib/strings']
[?pipe '  margherita  ' [$strings:trim] [$strings:upper]]"""]]

    [child id=tut-16 n=0.9.16 title='Multi-branch conditional'
      [body
        """`[?if]` is strictly two-branch — a condition, a `[then]`,
           an `[else]`. Multi-way dispatch on a value is `[?match]`:
           `[case]` arms carry a literal or pattern plus a result,
           tried in order, and `[else]` is the catch-all."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[?let [= $size 'medium']
             [?match $size
               [case 'small' 9] [case 'medium' 12] [case 'large' 15]
               [else 0]]]"""]]

    [child id=tut-17 n=0.9.17 title='Named templates'
      [body
        """`[?def]` defines a reusable fragment; call it like any
           other function — `[$line …]` — with positional args."""]
      [example lang=cx """[?def line ($item) [tr [td $item/@name] [td $item/@qty]]]"""]]

    [child id=tut-18 n=0.9.18 title='Includes'
      [body
        """`[?cx include=PATH]` splices another file's children into
           the parent document at parse time. Resolution is opt-in:
           without an include root the directive is preserved
           untouched; with `--include-root` every include resolves
           during the parse. Paths are relative-only and bounded by
           the root — no absolute paths, no URLs, no escaping the
           root — and include depth defaults to 8. Errors are the
           data-parse codes `cx-err:E901`–`E911`."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[users
             [?cx include=lib/admins.cx]
             [user name=carol]]"""]
      [example lang=shell """cx --xml --include-root=. config.cx"""]]

    [child id=tut-19 n=0.9.19 title='Identity'
      [body
        """Every CX document has a canonical form: presentation
           stripped, attribute order preserved as written, anchors
           resolved and erased (the merge is materialized).
           `cx hash` returns the SHA-256 of those
           canonical bytes. Two documents with the same data
           have the same hash, in any dialect."""]
      [example lang=shell """cx hash menu.cx"""]]

    [child id=tut-20 n=0.9.20 title='Putting it all together'
      [body
        """Every shape composes naturally with every other. The
           whole-shop example at §14.50 and the whole-kitchen CX
           code example at §15.50 exercise every shape in this
           tutorial inside a single working document."""]]]]
